<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">ʻAhu ʻula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ʻahuʻula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ʻahu ʻula, the feather cape or cloak of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, was with the mahiole, the feather helmet, a symbol of the highest rank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a>, the chiefly class. Made of a netting of olonā fibre to which small bundles of red, yellow and sometimes black and green feathers of native birds were tied, hundreds of thousands to a cloak, it was reserved for royals and high chiefs, and its size showed rank. It was believed to give spiritual protection and to take on the wearer's mana. In 1779 the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a> placed his cloak and helmet on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a>. Over 160 are in museums; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>'s cloak in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Museum holds 450,000 mamo feathers from 80,000 birds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahu-ula.jpg" alt="ʻAhu ʻula" width="330" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William T. Brigham Bishop Museum; derivative work by nagualdesign · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haalelea's_Feather_Cape.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
